From 6e3ac59b8f7994f988cf7f2c6362d4fc99c1233c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 02:13:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: libipa: agc_mean_luminance: Keep the target within
 the exposure limits

In the dark the luminance estimate is near zero and estimateInitialGain()
multiplies the gain by 10 on each of its 8 iterations. The exposure time
and gain stop at their maximums, but the filtered exposure value keeps
the target, up to 10^8 times what the sensor can do. When the light
comes back, each calculation shrinks that excess by the filter speed
only, and the exposure stays at its maximum until the filtered value is
back below it: seconds of a white image.

Clamp the target to the range of exposure time times analogue gain of
the exposure mode before filtering, and the filtered value too, so that
limits that shrink (a higher minimum frame rate, a manual gain) take
effect at once. None of the users of AgcMeanLuminance applies digital
gain.

After manual exposure or gain, the automatic exposure now ramps from the
manual value at the filter speed instead of jumping: the filtered value
stays pinned while the limits are a single value.

On the Huawei MateBook E Go's rear camera (ov13b10, soft ISP), lying on
a desk in the dark, the exposure did not move at all during 5 seconds
of flash LED light. With the clamp it starts on the next statistics,
0.13 s after the LED, and settles in about 2.3 s for a 130 times
brighter scene.

Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp b/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
index 34f21bcc..4ed0e1bd 100644
--- a/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/libipa/agc_mean_luminance.cpp
@@ -134,8 +134,9 @@ static constexpr unsigned int kDefaultLuxLevel = 500;
  * \class AgcMeanLuminance
  * \brief A mean-based auto-exposure algorithm
  *
- * This algorithm calculates an exposure time, analogue and digital gain such
- * that the normalised mean luminance value of an image is driven towards a
+ * This algorithm calculates an exposure time and analogue gain (plus a digital
+ * gain that only makes up for rounding) such that the normalised mean
+ * luminance value of an image is driven towards a
  * target, which itself is discovered from tuning data. The algorithm is a
  * two-stage process.
  *
@@ -154,8 +155,9 @@ static constexpr unsigned int kDefaultLuxLevel = 500;
  * stage is then clamped to the gain from this stage.
  *
  * The final gain is used to adjust the effective exposure value of the image,
- * and that new exposure value is divided into exposure time, analogue gain and
- * digital gain according to the selected AeExposureMode. This class uses the
+ * and that new exposure value, kept within the limits of exposure time and
+ * analogue gain, is divided into exposure time, analogue gain and digital gain
+ * according to the selected AeExposureMode. This class uses the
  * \ref ExposureModeHelper class to assist in that division, and expects the
  * data needed to initialise that class to be present in tuning data in a
  * format described in \ref parseTuningData().
@@ -662,8 +664,10 @@ utils::Duration AgcMeanLuminance::filterExposure(utils::Duration exposureValue,
  * \param[in] params The set of parameters for the exppsure value calculation
  *
  * Calculate a new exposure value to try to obtain the target. The calculated
- * exposure value is filtered to prevent rapid changes from frame to frame, and
- * divided into exposure time, analogue, quantization and digital gain.
+ * exposure value is kept within the limits of exposure time and analogue gain,
+ * filtered to prevent rapid changes from frame to frame, and divided into
+ * exposure time, analogue, quantization and digital gain. The digital gain
+ * makes up for rounding only.
  *
  * \return Tuple of exposure time, analogue gain, quantization gain and digital
  * gain
@@ -703,6 +707,23 @@ AgcMeanLuminance::calculateNewEv(const Params &params)
 
 	utils::Duration newExposureValue = params.effectiveExposureValue * gain;
 
+	/*
+	 * Keep the target within what exposure time and analogue gain can do.
+	 * The filter would otherwise hold a value the exposure cannot follow:
+	 * in the dark the gain estimate reaches 10^8, and when the light comes
+	 * back the filtered value takes seconds to come down to the maximum,
+	 * while the exposure stays there.
+	 */
+	const utils::Duration minExposureValue =
+		exposureModeHelper.minExposureTime() * exposureModeHelper.minGain();
+	const utils::Duration maxExposureValue =
+		exposureModeHelper.maxExposureTime() * exposureModeHelper.maxGain();
+	newExposureValue = std::clamp(newExposureValue, minExposureValue, maxExposureValue);
+	/* The limits change with the frame duration and manual controls too. */
+	if (frameCount_)
+		filteredExposure_ = std::clamp(filteredExposure_, minExposureValue,
+					       maxExposureValue);
+
 	/*
 	 * We filter the exposure value to make sure changes are not too jarring
 	 * from frame to frame.
